1
00:00:00,560 --> 00:00:05,560
Hey everyone, we're going to be taking a look at the gains from trade inside of a non strategic bargain.

2
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:10,880
And with that what we're talking about is looking at John Nash's axiom here.

3
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:12,560
So let's go ahead and dive into it.

4
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:23,960
And we have, formally speaking, we're going to let a be the agreement that is reached, whatever the two parties that come together and decide the price that one will pay and the other will receive D1 and D2.

5
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:29,600
Here are the disagreement values, that is the outside options that both of these players have.

6
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:38,320
Now to have positive gains from trade, we have to have the agreement reach minus the disagreement values be greater than zero.

7
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:45,920
And then when this is achieved and we have John Nash's axiom applied, we say that the gains are split evenly.

8
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:50,960
Otherwise we don't have a Nash bargaining solution.

9
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:53,560
That is we don't approach John Nash's axiom.

10
00:00:53,560 --> 00:00:57,920
So we have to have these gains split evenly in order to achieve that.

11
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:02,840
Now let's take a look at some of the concepts and intuition behind this.

12
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:08,400
Knowing how to bargain non strategically here is about changing the discriminant value of the player.

13
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:14,880
So if you have a better outside option, then you lean the bargain more in your favor.

14
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:20,800
If you want to increase your bargaining power to do this you need to do a few things.

15
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:24,320
One, increase your disagreement value, get a better outside option.

16
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:30,200
Two, decrease the other player's disagreement value, so decrease their outside options.

17
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:33,960
Or three, do a combination of both of those.

18
00:01:33,960 --> 00:01:46,920
So again it's all about changing the outside options that are available to disagreement values that are available for yourself and for the other player inside of the bargain that's taken place.

19
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:50,360
Now if you do any of these, then we can improve our bargaining position.

20
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:57,680
So what we're trying to do is, is make it more difficult for the other party in the bargain to have an improved position.

21
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:06,400
Now if you have the improved position, it allows you to more easily walk away than what the other player may have.

22
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:09,520
So the other player becomes more accepting to your terms.

23
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:25,840
So if you think about salary negotiations, if you have outside options, that is if you have a job offer elsewhere, then it becomes more difficult for your for the other party to bargain hard against you because you have the ability to walk away more easily.

24
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:27,920
So let's take a look at an example here.

25
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:31,040
And we're going to be looking at buying a used car.

26
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:32,440
We can haggle a little bit.

27
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:36,720
We're going to have the value of the car that you place on it at $15,000.

28
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:41,920
So this is your maximum willingness to pay and the dealer has a value on the car of 10,000.

29
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:44,480
So this is their minimum willingness to sell.

30
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:47,840
Okay, the dealer will sell that car at 10,000, but doesn't really want to.

31
00:02:47,840 --> 00:02:51,240
You would buy it at 15,000, but you really don't want to.

32
00:02:51,240 --> 00:02:53,920
You want to do some price lower than 15,000.

33
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:57,000
The dealer wants to do some price above 10,000.

34
00:02:57,880 --> 00:02:59,560
So here we look at a few scenarios.

35
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:09,080
One, we're trying to increase our or your disagreement value so we can find a car that's just like the one that we're bargaining for right now for $14,000.

36
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:14,600
Or we can look at the dealer saying, I got another person coming in willing to buy this car for 16.

37
00:03:16,040 --> 00:03:19,840
So we have two different scenarios here, and we're going to be asking ourselves a few questions.

38
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:23,800
One, what are the potential gains from a bargain between you and the dealer?

39
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:26,920
Two, what are the disagreement values?

40
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:31,880
And then three, what price represents a Nash bargaining solution?

41
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:40,280
Because remember, the Nash bargaining solution, we're trying to split the gains from trade to get a true Nash bargaining solution.

42
00:03:40,280 --> 00:03:41,640
So let's look at these answers here.

43
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:44,720
Consumers value $15,000.

44
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:50,160
This is our maximum willingness to pay or your maximum willingness to pay the dealer's minimum acceptable price.

45
00:03:50,160 --> 00:03:54,560
What they're willing to sell, they don't want to, but they will, is $10,000.

46
00:03:54,560 --> 00:04:01,200
So the maximum consumer surplus, assuming you could get the car at $10,000, would be five grand.

47
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,480
The minimum willingness to pay in scenario one was $14,000.

48
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:13,160
So potential joint gains from trade at a maximum that is, would be $4,000.

49
00:04:13,160 --> 00:04:15,960
That is the 10,000 up to the $14,000.

50
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:32,320
So if you have a disagreement value that lowers your willingness to pay from 15,000 to $14,000, and you buy that car at $10,000, the potential joint gains from trade are 10,000, or, excuse me, are $4,000.

51
00:04:32,320 --> 00:04:36,360
The issue with this is it all goes to you.

52
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:38,120
It doesn't go to the dealer.

53
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:49,920
Now, if we were to price at $12,000, then 2,000 goes to you, 2,000 goes to the dealer, and that becomes more in line with Nash's bargaining solution.

54
00:04:50,480 --> 00:05:00,960
Look at scenario two, alternative to sell at $16,000, you, the consumer, have a maximum willingness to pay of $15,000.  So guess what?

55
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:06,040
You're not going to be getting the car, and no deal is going to be made, and there are no potential gains.

56
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:09,240
Okay, let's look at this a little bit more in depth here.

57
00:05:09,720 --> 00:05:15,880
In the first scenario, again, our disagreement value, the value we get from buying a similar car elsewhere.

58
00:05:15,960 --> 00:05:18,520
So we had a maximum list pay of 15,000.

59
00:05:18,920 --> 00:05:27,000
But we find a car that's very similar to the one that we want to buy from this current dealer for 14,000, meaning we have $1,000 disagreement value.

60
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:34,560
This is what we can fall back to if we need to tell that first dealer, hey, look, I'm not paying more than 14,000 since I have outside options.

61
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:39,640
I have a car down the road just like this one here, that I can go purchase for $14,000.

62
00:05:39,640 --> 00:05:43,080
So you create that $1,000 cushion for yourself.

63
00:05:43,640 --> 00:05:49,320
Now, the dealer, on the other hand here, we still have 1,000 that we can fall back on our outside option.

64
00:05:49,560 --> 00:06:00,920
But the dealer now looks at this and goes, I can sell this car for $16,000 to somebody else, and my minimum willingness to sell is $10,000 what it costs to me to have this car.

65
00:06:01,640 --> 00:06:07,320
So I have a $6,000 disagreement value compared to your $1,000 disagreement value.

66
00:06:07,320 --> 00:06:17,520
So the dealer in this case has increased their outside options so they're more willing to sell to somebody who's going to pay the higher price compared to what you are willing to pay that lower price.

67
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:26,120
But just for the sake of argument, we're going to say that these two are going to get together and they're going to engage in trade, and they're going to have a little bargain.

68
00:06:26,440 --> 00:06:30,280
And in scenario one, we purchase that car for $14,000.

69
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:34,960
Now, the total surplus that's available, 15,000, minus 10,000.

70
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:41,040
What our maximum willingness to pay is, minus what the dealer's minimum willingness to sell is, is $5,000.

71
00:06:41,280 --> 00:06:43,400
In this scenario, we get 1,000.

72
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:45,440
From that 15,000.

73
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:52,240
Our maximum was to pay, minus our outside option, what we could have done, but we ended up getting the car to 14,000 anyway.

74
00:06:52,480 --> 00:06:53,880
So we get 1,000.

75
00:06:53,880 --> 00:07:01,200
From this, the dealer gets 4,000, what the dealer sold the car for, minus what their minimum willingness to sell was.

76
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:02,920
So they get 4,000.

77
00:07:02,920 --> 00:07:06,840
This isn't a Nash bargaining solution, since those gains aren't split evenly.

78
00:07:07,240 --> 00:07:15,960
In order to get a Nash bargaining solution, what we should do is instead of having the price be 14,000, the price should be 12,500.

79
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:20,440
In this case, each party gets 2,500 in surplus.

80
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:22,760
Now, that's scenario one.

81
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:25,640
Again, both had outside options, but they came together.

82
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:31,800
Excuse me, you, player one, had an outside option, which you came together.

83
00:07:32,120 --> 00:07:35,160
You were able to sell the car for 14,000.

84
00:07:35,960 --> 00:07:39,960
You did have a gain from trade, but didn't reach the full Nash bargaining solution.

85
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:45,800
And the better solution to achieve even gains from trade would have been to have a price be 12,500.

86
00:07:45,800 --> 00:07:48,600
Now, in the Second scenario, there's no deal.

87
00:07:49,160 --> 00:08:00,920
There's just no deal because the dealer has an outside option that's way greater than anything that you're ready to deal with because they can now sell that car for 16,000 versus you have the maximum willingness to pay of only 15,000.

88
00:08:01,240 --> 00:08:03,560
So that brings us to some key takeaways here.

89
00:08:04,120 --> 00:08:09,480
In this bargain, scenario two gave the dealer a much stronger position than what you had.

90
00:08:10,280 --> 00:08:14,120
The buyer thus had a lower opportunity cost, lower disagreement value.

91
00:08:14,680 --> 00:08:25,360
So if the scenario were to play out in real life, what we would have seen is that deal never would have took place because the dealer was in such a better position than the buyer was.

92
00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:31,400
So, but had the dealer had no potential buyers, then what we would have seen is their disagreement value decrease.

93
00:08:31,400 --> 00:08:44,080
And they would have been more willing to accept a deal at 14,000, maybe even lower, just to sell that car because there was surplus to be had in doing so.

94
00:08:44,720 --> 00:08:47,800
Now, had the buyer not had any alternative, then guess what?

95
00:08:47,800 --> 00:08:49,840
They wouldn't have had any disagreement value either.

96
00:08:50,280 --> 00:08:53,560
And so that decreases their bargaining power.

97
00:08:54,120 --> 00:08:55,800
Which brings us to a final point.

98
00:08:55,800 --> 00:09:04,520
Altering our disagreement value is the key to successfully, successfully getting a bargain to work in your favor.

99
00:09:04,760 --> 00:09:13,960
And if you take that knowledge and you apply it to your negotiations, you will have a better bargaining position in them.  And that's it.  Hope you've enjoyed it.